# CMake expands this template into the build tree's zlib.pc -- the
# configure_file() call in CMakeLists.txt -- and installs the result into
# <libdir>/pkgconfig.  The autotools build (configure plus Makefile.in) and
# win32/Makefile.gcc use the parallel template zlib.pc.in rather than this file,
# so a change to what either one advertises belongs in both.
#
# This descriptor is implementation-agnostic for DYNAMIC linking: the flags below
# describe the C implementation and the memory-safe Rust implementation
# (crates/libz-rs-sys) equally well there, so ZLIB_BUILD_RUST=ON needs no variant
# of this file and changes none of the keys below.  What the Rust packaging is
# REQUIRED to reproduce, and what the ZLIB_BUILD_RUST block in CMakeLists.txt
# implements:
#
#   * the staged artifacts are named libz.so and libz.a -- the same names the C
#     targets publish, and that block is ELF-only -- so -lz resolves to either;
#   * the shared object carries SONAME libz.so.1, and the names around it are laid
#     out exactly as the C build lays them out: the real file is
#     libz.so.1.3.2.1-motley, named from ZLIB_VERSION in zlib.h, and libz.so.1
#     and libz.so are two symlinks pointing DIRECTLY at it rather than one
#     resolving through the other.  A program already linked against libz then
#     keeps loading it under the name recorded in its DT_NEEDED entry.  This
#     build adds ONE further direct alias, and only when it is a different name:
#     if project()'s version spells the versioned file differently from the
#     header's -- libz.so.<PROJECT_VERSION> -- that name is symlinked onto the
#     same real file too, so an installed ZLIB-shared.cmake naming the C
#     library's file resolves here as well.  Cargo produces none of this: it
#     writes a bare libz.so, so CMakeLists.txt relinks cargo's static archive
#     into the versioned name -- with the same SONAME and the same zlib.map
#     version script the C shared target receives -- and creates those aliases in
#     a staging directory of its own rather than in cargo's output directory.
#     Those files install into the same libdir as the C libraries, from rules
#     declared last, so where both implementations publish a name the Rust file
#     is the one installed;
#   * the exported symbol surface must match the C library's 111 dynamic
#     globals, being 95 functions plus the 16 symbol-version nodes declared in
#     zlib.map.  The CMake path takes that keep-list from the ZEXTERN
#     declarations in zlib.h, localises everything else with objcopy before the
#     shared link, and hands zlib.map to the linker.  The nm diff of that same
#     property runs on the autotools artifacts and on cargo's own -- the `rust'
#     target, `make rust-symbols', the symbol-parity job of
#     .github/workflows/rust.yml, and `crates/libz-rs-sys/tests/symbol_parity.rs'
#     inside `cargo test' -- and NOT on the library this block stages, whose
#     keep-list is enforced by construction with objcopy instead.  So for this
#     build read it as the parity requirement, and run `make rust-symbols' on the
#     autotools staging to see it measured;
#   * the installed zlib.h and generated zconf.h are byte-identical, because the
#     Rust build modifies neither header and that block leaves the header, the
#     zlib.pc and the documentation install rules untouched.
#
# Libs: advertising -lz and Cflags: advertising the include directory are part of
# that contract -- do not reword them, and do not add Requires: entries, -lm,
# -lpthread or any Rust-specific flag to them by hand.
#
# ONE PACKAGING DIFFERENCE, AND IT IS REAL: STATIC LINKING.
#
# The C libz.a needs no companion libraries.  A Rust staticlib is different: it
# carries the Rust standard library's own object code, whose undefined references
# reach into libc and its neighbours, and nothing in the archive records that.  On
# x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu the set measured for this tree is
#
#   -lgcc_s -lutil -lrt -lpthread -lm -ldl -lc
#
# but that is an illustration, not a constant: the set is TARGET-SPECIFIC and
# rustc is the only thing that knows it, so it is not written here.  CMakeLists.txt
# runs `rustc --crate-type staticlib --print native-static-libs' at configure time
# against a throwaway staticlib -- the only invocation that makes rustc answer --
# refuses to configure if it cannot get an answer, and substitutes the result into
# the single @-delimited name at the end of the Libs: line below.
#
# THERE IS EXACTLY ONE SUCH NAME IN THIS FILE, AND EXACTLY ONE OWNER OF THE KEY IT
# WRITES.  It expands to a complete additional line, `Libs.private: <set>', in Rust
# mode and to NOTHING AT ALL in C mode.  Folding the newline into the substituted
# value, rather than leaving a bare `Libs.private:' key in the template with a
# possibly-empty value after it, is what leaves the C build's key set exactly what
# it always was: a descriptor with no such key, which is the truth for an archive
# that stands alone.  One template therefore serves both implementations honestly.
#
# Do not add a second occurrence of the name, and do not add the key from anywhere
# else either.  Two occurrences write the key twice; pkg-config reads the first and
# ignores the rest, so the descriptor stops saying what it appears to say -- and a
# second producer downstream of configure_file() cannot even tell whether the key
# it is about to add is already there.  This file is where the key comes from.
#
# Dynamic linking never needed any of it: the shared object records its own
# dependencies in DT_NEEDED, so -lz alone is correct there in both modes -- which
# is why the substitution appends to Libs: rather than altering it.
#
# The CMake build additionally publishes the same set on the exported
# ZLIB::ZLIBSTATIC target, so a consumer reaching the library through
# find_package(ZLIB CONFIG) gets a complete link line without going through
# pkg-config at all.
#
# zlib.pc.in, the template `configure' expands for the autotools build, has no
# ZLIB_BUILD_RUST equivalent to key off: configure runs before anyone decides
# whether to run `make rust'.  That path therefore derives the same set inside the
# `rust' target and writes it into a descriptor of its own beside the staged
# library, which `make rust-pc' gates and prints the install command for; see
# zlib.pc.in and the `rust-pc' target in Makefile.in.
#
# Two things about the substitution, for whoever edits this next.  The
# configure_file() call passes @ONLY, so only the at-delimited names are
# substituted and the ${...} references in libdir, sharedlibdir and includedir
# survive into the generated file, for pkg-config itself to resolve at use time
# against the prefix recorded there -- so those three follow whatever prefix line
# ends up in the generated descriptor.  Do not expand them by hand and do not drop
# that keyword.
#
# WHERE THE PREFIX COMES FROM, AND WHO CORRECTS IT.  The two prefix lines below are
# written from the configure-time CMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX by the substitution, and
# CMake does not regenerate a configure_file() output at install time.  On its own
# that made the descriptor non-relocatable: `cmake --install . --prefix
# /somewhere/else` installed a .pc still naming the configure-time prefix, and
# `pkg-config --cflags zlib` then answered an include directory that did not exist.
#
# CMakeLists.txt now closes that at the only place that knows the answer.  An
# install(CODE ...) rule declared beside the zlib.pc install rule rewrites the
# `prefix=` and `exec_prefix=` lines of the INSTALLED copy to the prefix the install
# actually used, and honours DESTDIR the way CMake's own generated script does: the
# file is read and written under $ENV{DESTDIR} while the prefix written INTO it is
# the prefix without DESTDIR, because a staged install is meant to be unpacked at
# the prefix rather than to advertise the staging directory.  Measured: an
# alternate-prefix install reports `-- Rewriting prefix in: <alt>/lib/pkgconfig/
# zlib.pc` and pkg-config then answers -I<alt>/include and -L<alt>/lib, and a
# DESTDIR install of --prefix /opt/zlibrust writes the file under DESTDIR carrying
# prefix=/opt/zlibrust.
#
# So: the two lines below are a DEFAULT that a correct install overwrites, not a
# binding. Keep them as plain absolute assignments -- the rewrite matches
# `(^|\n)prefix=[^\n]*`, so a `${pcfiledir}`-relative form or a line moved out of
# column 0 would silently stop being corrected.  Only the three ${...} derivations
# were ever relocatable by themselves, and they still follow whatever prefix line
# the installed file ends up carrying.  The configure-generated zlib.pc from
# zlib.pc.in is a separate file with its own prefix, taken from ./configure's
# --prefix, and is unaffected by any of this.
#
# And the substitution pass runs over the whole file, comments included: never
# write one of the substitutable names in here, because it would be rewritten here
# too, and one CMake happens not to define expands to nothing at all rather than
# failing.
#
# A KNOWN DIVERGENCE FROM zlib.pc.in, DELIBERATE AND OUT OF SCOPE TO HARMONISE:
# the Version: field below is filled from the version given to project() in
# CMakeLists.txt, which is 1.3.2.1, whereas the configure-generated zlib.pc
# carries ZLIB_VERSION from zlib.h, which is 1.3.2.1-motley.  Both spellings
# predate the Rust port.  The suffixed one is the library's version identity --
# deflateInit_ and inflateInit_ compare a caller's compile-time copy of it --
# while project() accepts numeric components only, so the two cannot be spelled
# alike without changing one of them.  Nothing links differently for it:
# pkg-config only reports this field and answers version queries from it, and
# the Rust staging above publishes the project()-spelled library name alongside
# the header-spelled one, so which spelling a consumer saw never decides which
# file resolves.

prefix=@CMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX@
exec_prefix=@CMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX@
libdir=${exec_prefix}/@CMAKE_INSTALL_LIBDIR@
sharedlibdir=${exec_prefix}/@CMAKE_INSTALL_LIBDIR@
includedir=${exec_prefix}/@CMAKE_INSTALL_INCLUDEDIR@

Name: zlib
Description: zlib compression library
Version: @zlib_VERSION@
License: Zlib

Requires:
Libs: -L${libdir} -L${sharedlibdir} -lz@ZLIB_PC_LIBS_PRIVATE@
Cflags: -I${includedir}
